"insight": "The sequence either reaches 1 or enters a <b>cycle</b>. Remember every number seen in a set: seeing one again means a cycle, so not happy. (Floyd's fast/slow pointers also works with O(1) space.)",
"steps": [
"Helper: sum of squares of digits using <code>divmod(x, 10)</code>.",
"Loop while <code>n != 1</code> and <code>n</code> not seen: add it to the set and move to the next number.",
"Return <code>n == 1</code>."
],
"code": "class Solution:\n    def isHappy(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        def next_num(x):\n            total = 0\n            while x:\n                x, d = divmod(x, 10)\n                total += d * d\n            return total\n\n        seen = set()\n        while n != 1 and n not in seen:\n            seen.add(n)\n            n = next_num(n)\n        return n == 1",
"time": "O(log n)",
"space": "O(log n)",
"cxWhy": "Numbers shrink quickly to at most three digits, so only a few steps happen.",
"run": "Solution().isHappy(19)",
"expect": "True",
