"149": {
"id": 149,
"slug": "max-points-on-a-line",
"title": "Max Points on a Line",
"diff": "Hard",
"topic": "hashing",
"pattern": "Slopes in a hash map",
"hook": "Given points on a plane, what is the largest number of them on one straight line?",
"brute": "For every pair, define the line and count points on it: O(n³).",
"insight": "Fix an anchor point. Every other point on a common line through the anchor has the same <b>slope</b>. Count slopes in a dict. Store the slope as a reduced fraction <code>(dy, dx)</code> with a fixed sign (divide by the gcd), avoiding floating point errors.",
"steps": [
"For each anchor i, count slopes to every later point j.",
"Slope key: divide (dx, dy) by their gcd; make dx positive (or dx = 0, dy positive).",
"The best count for a key + 1 (the anchor) is a candidate answer."
],
"code": "class Solution:\n    def maxPoints(self, points: List[List[int]]) -> int:\n        n = len(points)\n        if n <= 2:\n            return n\n        best = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            slopes = defaultdict(int)\n            x1, y1 = points[i]\n            for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                dx = points[j][0] - x1\n                dy = points[j][1] - y1\n                g = math.gcd(dx, dy)\n                dx, dy = dx // g, dy // g            # reduced fraction\n                if dx < 0 or (dx == 0 and dy < 0):\n                    dx, dy = -dx, -dy                # one sign for the same line\n                slopes[(dx, dy)] += 1\n                best = max(best, slopes[(dx, dy)] + 1)   # + the anchor\n        return best",
"time": "O(n² log C)",
"space": "O(n)",
"cxWhy": "log C for the gcd of coordinate differences.",
"run": "Solution().maxPoints([[1, 1], [3, 2], [5, 3], [4, 1], [2, 3], [1, 4]])",
"expect": "4",
"tip": "Using floating slopes (dy / dx) risks precision bugs and needs special handling for vertical lines. The reduced-fraction key avoids both.",
"plans": [
"top150"
],
"traceSteps": 159
}
